.Dd October 26, 2026
.Dt DOOR_SHM_PAIR 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm door_shm_pair_create ,
.Nm door_shm_pair_attach ,
.Nm door_shm_pair_load ,
.Nm door_shm_pair_notify ,
.Nm door_shm_pair_wait ,
.Nm door_shm_pool_create ,
.Nm door_shm_pool_region ,
.Nm door_shm_pool_reserve ,
.Nm door_shm_pool_release ,
.Nm door_shm_pool_destroy ,
.Nm door_shm_chain_map
.Nd paired SHM events and variable-length allocation chains
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libdoor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/uio.h
.In door.h
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_pair_create "int fd" "size_t size" "door_shm_t *shm"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_pair_attach "int fd" "door_shm_id_t region" "door_shm_t *shm"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_pair_load "const door_shm_t *shm" "uint_t lane" "uint32_t *value"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_pair_notify "const door_shm_t *shm" "uint_t lane"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_pair_wait "const door_shm_t *shm" "uint_t lane" "uint32_t expected" "uint64_t timeout_ns"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_pool_create "int fd" "size_t bytes" "uint_t max_leases" "door_shm_pool_t **pool"
.Ft "const door_shm_t *"
.Fn door_shm_pool_region "const door_shm_pool_t *pool"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_pool_reserve "door_shm_pool_t *pool" "size_t bytes" "door_shm_chain_t *chain"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_pool_release "door_shm_pool_t *pool" "const door_shm_chain_t *chain"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_pool_destroy "door_shm_pool_t *pool"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_chain_map "const door_shm_t *shm" "const door_shm_chain_t *chain" "struct iovec *iov" "uint_t capacity"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These optional interfaces extend persistent Door SHM.
They do not alter CALL, ONEWAY, native async, ring, or page-view contracts.
.Pp
A pair region reserves its first VM page for control information.
Two aligned 32-bit sequence words occupy offsets 0 and 64.
Lanes 0 and 1 are independent application-defined events.
The creator and the Door owner may share the region; a third process cannot
attach or operate on its wait queues merely by knowing the region id.
Fork-inherited mappings remain inherited mappings; this interface does not
remove memory access deliberately inherited through
.Xr fork 2 .
.Pp
.Fn door_shm_pair_create
requires read/write memory and at least one VM page.
.Fn door_shm_pair_attach
requires the calling process to own the Door and requests read/write access.
Use
.Fn door_shm_destroy_handle
followed by
.Fn door_shm_unmap
to clean up a directly created pair region.
An attached peer only unmaps its local handle.
.Pp
.Fn door_shm_pair_load
performs an acquire load without entering the kernel.
.Fn door_shm_pair_notify
increments a sequence with release ordering, then enters the kernel to wake
all waiters for that region and lane.
Notify once per published batch, after publishing the actual queue predicate.
A failed wake does not undo the shared sequence increment.
.Pp
.Fn door_shm_pair_wait
returns immediately if the sequence has changed.
Otherwise the kernel rechecks the wired VM-object word while registering an
interruptible wait under the same lock used by wake and invalidation.
The timeout is relative, in nanoseconds; zero polls and
.Dv DOOR_SHM_PAIR_FOREVER
waits indefinitely.
Finite values are limited to one day.
Spurious wakes do not extend the original deadline.
Success means the event sequence changed; it does not mean a request completed.
A memory-only mismatch may succeed after revoke because it does not inspect
kernel state.
.Pp
The caller must load the sequence before rechecking its empty-queue or
no-free-space predicate, then wait only if that predicate still holds.
Sequence words must not be reset while participants are active.
A complete 32-bit wrap between observation and wait can cause ABA; applications
must bound that interval or change their higher-level generation.
.Pp
Destroy, Door revoke, or exit/exec of a participant invalidates the pair and
wakes its registered waiters.
Retained mappings are not forcibly removed.
Unmap only after joining local users of those mappings.
.Sh ALLOCATION CHAINS
.Fn door_shm_pool_create
creates a pair region with at least
.Fa bytes
payload bytes, rounded to 64 bytes, plus one control page.
The number of simultaneous leases is explicitly bounded by
.Fa max_leases
in the range 1 through 4096.
Allocation metadata and the mutex reside in the creator's private memory.
The returned pool is process-local and cannot be reused after fork.
.Pp
.Fn door_shm_pool_region
returns a borrowed handle for registration, attachment and event operations.
Do not destroy or unmap this borrowed handle independently.
.Fn door_shm_pool_reserve
returns a variable-length chain containing at most 64 offset/length segments.
No payload is copied.
Failure consumes neither memory nor a lease.
.Pp
.Fn door_shm_chain_map
validates a local snapshot of the descriptor and produces process-local
.Vt "struct iovec"
entries.
It rejects wrong region/generation, overlap, control-page access, invalid
counts, overflow, or an inconsistent total length.
It does not authenticate the lease against the producer's private allocator
or prove that the producer has not released it.
The surrounding protocol must provide publication and a final-access
acknowledgement before reuse.
.Pp
.Fn door_shm_pool_release
requires the exact issued descriptor, clears its rounded payload extents,
returns the extents to the allocator, and coalesces adjacent free space.
Old or modified descriptors cannot free a different lease.
Clearing is a memory-bandwidth cost; zero-copy does not mean zero stores.
.Pp
Reserve and release are serialized within a pool and support multiple threads.
Destroy requires exclusive local access after joining all local users and
returns
.Er EBUSY
while leases remain.
There is no automatic release on cancellation or timeout: a peer might still
be accessing the payload.
.Pp
This first interface provides neither an ordered descriptor queue, kqueue
readiness, an inter-channel global allocator, nor mirrored VA mappings.
The application supplies its descriptor-publication protocol.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Integer functions return zero on success and -1 with
.Va errno
on error.
.Fn door_shm_pool_region
returns NULL on error.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ETIMEDOUT
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
Insufficient free payload, excessive fragmentation, or no free lease.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
A Door SHM quota or the global 1024-pair control-page budget is exhausted.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Destroy attempted while leases remain, or an incompatible SHM operation.
.It Bq Er ESTALE
A release descriptor is old, unknown, or modified.
.It Bq Er ETIMEDOUT
The sequence did not change before the wait deadline.
.It Bq Er EINTR
An interruptible wait was interrupted by a signal.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The region was destroyed or invalidated by participant exit/exec.
.It Bq Er EBADF
The Door was revoked or its capability is invalid.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The process is not a registered participant or is using an inherited allocator.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
An argument, descriptor, flags, lane, or timeout is invalid.
.It Bq Er EOVERFLOW
A requested size or allocation generation cannot be represented.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr door 3 ,
